-opt.wrong{border-color:var(--red)!important;background:rgba(239,68,68,.1)!important;color:var(--red)!important}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:12px;padding:12px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.84em;color:var(--text-sec);line-height:1.6}.q-soln.show{display:block}</style></head><body><div class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><div class="brand-text">vlymbooq</div><div class="nav-links"><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a></div></div></div><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Quantum Optics — Lasers, Stimulated Emission, Cavity QED, Photon Statistics &amp; Squeezed Light</h2><p>Deep dive into Quantum Optics — the quantum theory of light, the Einstein coefficients for spontaneous and stimulated emission, the laser threshold condition and the rate equations, the Purcell effect in cavity quantum electrodynamics (cavity QED), the photon statistics of coherent, thermal, and Fock states including the Hanbury Brown–Twiss effect and photon antibunching, and squeezed states of light where quantum noise is reduced below the vacuum level in one quadrature at the expense of increased noise in the conjugate quadrature.</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Light is not merely a wave or a particle — it is a quantum field whose statistical properties reveal the deepest structure of the electromagnetic vacuum."</div><div class="attribution">— Roy J. Glauber</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>💡 Lasers and Stimulated Emission</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Laser Physics</div><div class="story-title">Einstein Coefficients, Population Inversion, and the Laser Threshold</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Einstein coefficients</strong> describe light–matter interaction: <em>A</em>₂₁ (spontaneous emission rate), <em>B</em>₁₂ (absorption rate coefficient), and <em>B</em>₂₁ (stimulated emission rate coefficient). The rates satisfy: <em>A</em>₂₁/<em>B</em>₂₁ = 2<em>h</em>ω³/π²<em>c</em>³ and <em>B</em>₁₂ = <em>B</em>₂₁ (degeneracy-weighted). The <strong>gain coefficient</strong> γ(ω) = σ(<em>N</em>₂ − <em>N</em>₁) where σ is the transition cross-section and <em>N</em>₂, <em>N</em>₁ are the populations of the upper and lower laser levels. <strong>Population inversion</strong> (<em>N</em>₂ > <em>N</em>₁) is required for net amplification. The <strong>laser threshold</strong> condition: the round-trip gain equals the round-trip loss: <em>R</em>₁<em>R</em>₂ exp(2(γ − α)<em>L</em>) = 1, where <em>R</em>₁,₂ are mirror reflectivities, γ is the gain per unit length, α is the distributed loss, and <em>L</em> is the cavity length. The <strong>rate equations</strong>: d<em>N</em>₂/d<em>t</em> = <em>R</em>ₚ − <em>N</em>₂/τ₂ − σ<em>c</em>(<em>N</em>₂ − <em>N</em>₁)φ and dφ/d<em>t</em> = σ<em>c</em>(<em>N</em>₂ − <em>N</em>₁)φ − φ/τₚ + β<em>N</em>₂/τ₂, where φ is the photon density, <em>R</em>ₚ is the pump rate, τ₂ is the upper-state lifetime, τₚ is the photon lifetime, and β is the spontaneous emission coupling factor. The <strong>Schawlow-Townes linewidth</strong>: Δν = (π<em>h</em>ν (Δν_c)²)/<em>P</em> where Δν_c is the cold cavity linewidth and <em>P</em> is the output power.</div><div class="story-moral">Stimulated emission provides optical amplification; the laser threshold is reached when gain overcomes all cavity losses, producing coherent light with an ultranarrow linewidth.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>🔬 Cavity QED</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Cavity QED</div><div class="story-title">The Purcell Effect, Strong Coupling, and the Jaynes-Cummings Model</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Cavity quantum electrodynamics</strong> studies the modification of spontaneous emission when an atom is placed inside a resonant optical cavity. The <strong>Purcell effect</strong>: the spontaneous emission rate is enhanced by the <strong>Purcell factor</strong> <em>F</em>ₚ = 3<em>Q</em>λ³/(4π²<em>V</em>_mode), where <em>Q</em> is the cavity quality factor and <em>V</em>_mode is the mode volume. The <strong>Jaynes-Cummings model</strong> describes a single two-level atom coupled to a single cavity mode: <em>H</em> = ℏω<em>a</em>†<em>a</em> + ½ℏω₀σ_z + ℏ<em>g</em>(σ₊<em>a</em> + σ₋<em>a</em>†), where <em>g</em> is the atom–field coupling strength. The <strong>Rabi frequency</strong> Ω = 2<em>g</em>√(<em>n</em>+1) for <em>n</em> photons. <strong>Strong coupling</strong> occurs when <em>g</em> > κ, γ (where κ is the cavity decay rate and γ is the atomic decay rate). In this regime, the system exhibits <strong>vacuum Rabi splitting</strong> — two peaks in the transmission spectrum separated by 2<em>g</em>. The <strong>Dicke model</strong> extends the Jaynes-Cummings model to many atoms, exhibiting the <strong>superradiant phase transition</strong>. Cavity QED enables <strong>quantum gates</strong> between atoms mediated by the cavity field, and is a platform for <strong>quantum networks</strong> where stationary qubits (atoms) interface with flying qubits (photons). The <strong>Rydberg blockade</strong> in cavity QED provides strong nonlinearities at the single-photon level.</div><div class="story-moral">Cavity QED controls the fundamental process of spontaneous emission by modifying the electromagnetic environment; strong coupling enables coherent quantum interactions between atoms and single photons.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>📊 Photon Statistics</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Statistics</div><div class="story-title">Coherent, Thermal, and Fock States; Hanbury Brown–Twiss; Antibunching</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>photon statistics</strong> of a light field are characterized by the <strong>second-order correlation function</strong> <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(τ) = ⟨:<em>I</em>(<em>t</em>)<em>I</em>(<em>t</em>+τ):⟩/⟨<em>I</em>(<em>t</em>)⟩². For a <strong>coherent state</strong> |α⟩ (the output of an ideal laser): <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(0) = 1 (Poissonian statistics, ⟨(Δ<em>n</em>)²⟩ = ⟨<em>n</em>⟩). For <strong>thermal (chaotic) light</strong>: <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(0) = 2 (bunched, super-Poissonian, ⟨(Δ<em>n</em>)²⟩ = ⟨<em>n</em>⟩ + ⟨<em>n</em>⟩²). For a <strong>Fock state</strong> |<em>n</em>⟩ (number state), <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(0) = 1 − 1/<em>n</em> (sub-Poissonian statistics). The <strong>Hanbury Brown–Twiss effect</strong> measures intensity correlations to determine the size of a thermal source: the correlation width is inversely related to the angular size of the source. <strong>Photon antibunching</strong> occurs when <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(τ) rises from zero at τ = 0 — the probability of detecting two photons simultaneously is less than detecting them separated in time. This is a purely quantum effect with no classical analogue. The <strong>Mandel <em>Q</em> parameter</strong>: <em>Q</em> = ⟨(Δ<em>n</em>)²⟩/⟨<em>n</em>⟩ − 1. For Poissonian <em>Q</em> = 0, for super-Poissonian <em>Q</em> > 0, and for sub-Poissonian <em>Q</em> < 0 (quantum light). The <strong>photon number distribution</strong> <em>p</em>(<em>n</em>) = |⟨<em>n</em>|ψ⟩|² for a quantum state can be measured using <strong>photon counting</strong> with avalanche photodiodes or superconducting nanowire detectors.</div><div class="story-moral">Photon statistics reveal the quantum nature of light: thermal light is bunched, laser light is Poissonian, and only quantum sources exhibit antibunching and sub-Poissonian statistics.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>🌀 Squeezed Light</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Squeezing</div><div class="story-title">Quadrature Squeezing, the Uncertainty Principle, and Applications</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Squeezed light</strong> is a quantum state of the electromagnetic field where the <strong>uncertainty</strong> in one <strong>quadrature</strong> component is reduced below the vacuum (zero-point) level, at the expense of increased uncertainty in the conjugate quadrature, preserving the Heisenberg uncertainty relation Δ<em>X</em>₁ Δ<em>X</em>₂ ≥ 1 (in normalized units, where Δ<em>X</em>₁ᵛᵃᶜ = Δ<em>X</em>₂ᵛᵃᶜ = 1 for the vacuum). The <strong>squeezing operator</strong> <em>S</em>(ξ) = exp(½ξ*<em>a</em>² − ½ξ<em>a</em>†²) with ξ = <em>r</em> exp(<em>i</em>θ) creates a squeezed vacuum state from the vacuum. The <strong>quadrature variances</strong> for a squeezed state are Δ<em>X</em>₁² = <em>e</em>^{-2<em>r</em>} and Δ<em>X</em>₂² = <em>e</em>^{2<em>r</em>}. Squeezed light is generated by <strong>degenerate parametric down-conversion</strong> in a χ⁽²⁾ nonlinear crystal inside an optical cavity (optical parametric oscillator, OPO). The <strong>squeezing spectrum</strong> describes the frequency dependence of the noise reduction. <strong>Amplitude squeezing</strong> reduces intensity noise below the shot-noise limit, while <strong>phase squeezing</strong> reduces phase noise. Applications include: <strong>gravitational wave detection</strong> (LIGO uses squeezed light to reduce quantum noise and enhance sensitivity), <strong>quantum metrology</strong> beyond the standard quantum limit (Heisenberg-limited interferometry), and <strong>continuous-variable quantum information</strong> (quantum key distribution, quantum teleportation of continuous variables). The <strong>Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen (EPR) paradox</strong> is realized using two-mode squeezed states (entangled beams produced by non-degenerate parametric down-conversion).</div><div class="story-moral">Squeezed light redistributes quantum noise between conjugate observables, enabling measurement sensitivity beyond the vacuum-noise limit — a key resource for quantum-enhanced metrology.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">🎯 Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">🔄 Reset All</button><a href="physics.html" class="btn btn-success">← Back to Physics Map</a></div></div></div><script>
var practiceQs=[{id:45253,text:"💡 The Einstein coefficient relation between spontaneous and stimulated emission rates is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"A₂₁/B₂₁ = 2hω³/π²c³",c:true},{l:"B",t:"A₂₁ = B₂₁",c:false},{l:"C",t:"A₂₁/B₂₁ = c³/2hω³",c:false},{l:"D",t:"A₂₁ B₂₁ = 1",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Einstein relation</strong>: <em>A</em>₂₁/<em>B</em>₂₁ = 2<em>h</em>ω³/π²<em>c</em>³. The spontaneous emission rate increases as the cube of the frequency."},{id:45254,text:"💡 The laser threshold condition requires:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Round-trip gain equals round-trip loss",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Population of lower level exceeds upper level",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Spontaneous emission is eliminated",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Cavity length equals the wavelength",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>threshold condition</strong> is <em>R</em>₁<em>R</em>₂ exp(2(γ − α)<em>L</em>) = 1: the round-trip gain exactly balances the round-trip loss."},{id:45255,text:"💡 Population inversion means:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"N₂ > N₁",c:true},{l:"B",t:"N₁ > N₂",c:false},{l:"C",t:"N₂ = N₁",c:false},{l:"D",t:"N₂ = 0",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Population inversion</strong> requires the upper laser level population <em>N</em>₂ to exceed the lower level population <em>N</em>₁, which is necessary for net optical gain."},{id:45256,text:"🔬 The Purcell factor for spontaneous emission enhancement in a cavity is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"F_p = 3Qλ³/(4π²V_mode)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"F_p = Q/V_mode",c:false},{l:"C",t:"F_p = 4π²V_mode/(3Qλ³)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"F_p = λ²/Q",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Purcell factor</strong> <em>F</em>ₚ = 3<em>Q</em>λ³/(4π²<em>V</em>_mode). A high <em>Q</em>/<em>V</em> ratio dramatically enhances spontaneous emission."},{id:45257,text:"🔬 In the Jaynes-Cummings model, the coupling term is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"ℏg(σ₊a + σ₋a†)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"ℏg(a + a†)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"ℏgσₓ",c:false},{l:"D",t:"ℏg(a†a)",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Jaynes-Cummings interaction</strong> Hamiltonian is ℏ<em>g</em>(σ₊<em>a</em> + σ₋<em>a</em>†), describing the resonant exchange of a quantum between the atom and the cavity mode."},{id:45258,text:"🔬 Strong coupling in cavity QED requires:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"g > κ, γ",c:true},{l:"B",t:"κ > g",c:false},{l:"C",t:"γ > g",c:false},{l:"D",t:"g = 0",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Strong coupling</strong> is achieved when the atom–field coupling <em>g</em> exceeds both the cavity decay rate κ and the atomic decay rate γ, enabling coherent Rabi oscillations."},{id:45259,text:"📊 For a coherent state (ideal laser), g⁽²⁾(0) equals:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"1",c:true},{l:"B",t:"2",c:false},{l:"C",t:"0",c:false},{l:"D",t:"½",c:false}],sol:"For a <strong>coherent state</strong> |α⟩, <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(0) = 1, indicating <strong>Poissonian</strong> photon statistics where ⟨(Δ<em>n</em>)²⟩ = ⟨<em>n</em>⟩."},{id:45260,text:"📊 Photon antibunching is characterized by:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"g⁽²⁾(0) < g⁽²⁾(τ) for small τ",c:true},{l:"B",t:"g⁽²⁾(0) > g⁽²⁾(τ)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"g⁽²⁾(0) = 2",c:false},{l:"D",t:"g⁽²⁾(0) = 1",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Antibunching</strong> occurs when <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(0) < <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(τ) for small τ — a single-photon source shows zero probability of detecting two photons simultaneously."},{id:45261,text:"📊 Thermal (chaotic) light has g⁽²⁾(0) = 2, which corresponds to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Bunched or super-Poissonian statistics",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Sub-Poissonian statistics",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Poissonian statistics",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Coherent statistics",c:false}],sol:"Thermal light is <strong>bunched</strong> with <em>g</em>⁽²⁾(0) = 2, meaning photons tend to arrive in clusters — <strong>super-Poissonian</strong> statistics with ⟨(Δ<em>n</em>)²⟩ > ⟨<em>n</em>⟩."},{id:45262,text:"🌀 Squeezed light has noise in one quadrature:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Below the vacuum (zero-point) level",c:true},{l:"B",t:"At the vacuum level",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Above the vacuum level in both quadratures",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Eliminated entirely",c:false}],sol:"A <strong>squeezed state</strong> reduces quantum noise in one quadrature below the vacuum level (Δ<em>X</em>₁² = <em>e</em>^{-2<em>r</em>} < 1) at the expense of increased noise in the conjugate quadrature."},{id:45263,text:"🌀 Squeezed light is commonly generated by:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Degenerate parametric down-conversion in a χ⁽²⁾ crystal",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Thermal emission from a blackbody",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Spontaneous emission from an LED",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Linear absorption in a neutral density filter",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Squeezed light</strong> is generated by <strong>degenerate parametric down-conversion</strong> in a χ⁽²⁾ nonlinear crystal inside an optical cavity (optical parametric oscillator)."},{id:45264,text:"🌀 A practical application of squeezed light is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Gravitational wave detection (LIGO)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Room illumination",c:false},{l:"C",t:"CD/DVD reading",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Fiber-optic telecommunications",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Squeezed light</strong> is used in <strong>gravitational wave detectors</strong> like LIGO to reduce quantum noise and improve the sensitivity of the interferometer beyond the standard quantum limit."}];var answered={},correctCount=0;function renderPractice(){var c=document.getElementById('practice-qs'),h='';for(var i=0;i<practiceQs.length;i++){var q=practiceQs[i],o='';for(var j=0;j<q.opts.length;j++){o+='<div class="q-opt" onclick="selectOpt('+q.id+','+j+')">'+q.opts[j].l+'. '+q.opts[j].t+'</div>';}h+='<div class="q-card" id="q-'+q.id+'"><div class="q-text">'+(i+1)+'. '+q.text+'</div><div class="q-opts">'+o+'</div><div class="q-soln" id="soln-'+q.id+'"><strong>'+getCorrectLabel(q)+'</strong><br>'+q.sol+'</div></div>';}c.innerHTML=h;}function getCorrectLabel(q){for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)return q.opts[i].l+'. '+q.opts[i].t;}return '';}function selectOpt(qId,idx){if(answered[qId])return;answered[qId]=true;var q=practiceQs.filter(function(x){return x.id===qId})[0];var opts=document.querySelectorAll('#q-'+qId+' .q-opt'),isCorrect=q.opts[idx].c;for(var i=0;i<opts.length;i++)opts[i].classList.add('disabled');if(isCorrect){opts[idx].classList.add('correct');correctCount++;}else{opts[idx].classList.add('wrong');for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)opts[i].classList.add('correct');}}document.getElementById('soln-'+qId).classList.add('show');}function resetPractice(){if(!confirm('Reset all answers?'))return;answered={};correctCount=0;document.querySelectorAll('.q-opt').forEach(function(e){e.className='q-opt'});document.querySelectorAll('.q-soln').forEach(function(e){e.classList.remove('show')});renderPractice();}renderPractice();</script></body></html>
